This file documents how to go about developing and debugging GEMDOS.

The way you develop GEMDOS is to create DOS.SYS, copy it to a special
boot disk, and cold-boot your system using that disk.  This will cause
DOS.SYS to replace the ROM GEMDOS.

Creating DOS.SYS is easy: say 'make'.  The first thing this does is
create gemdos.o, just like the ROMs use.  It then slaps some glue to the
beginning of it to generate DOS.SYS. 

The glue is self.o, ld_dos.o, announce.o, clock.o, and clear.o,
along with fsstub.o, the debugger stub for the file system.

self.o must be the first file in the link: it gets control after the
boot sector on the disk loaded it.  It performs fixups on its own image,
just like Pexec does.

ld_dos.o must be the second file: when self.o is done, it falls right
through to the next object file.  ld_dos.o provides some externals which
GEMDOS needs, installs the debugger stub if you ask for it, and displays
the logic-hazard announcement with the version date.  Then it calls
_osi, the initialization entry point for GEMDOS.  ld_dos also
installs a reset handler which clears _bootdev when you hit reset.
This is becase 11/20 and 4/22 ROMs don't check the floppy for a
warm boot when you have a bootable hard disk.

Note that if you DO have a bootable hard disk, it will work as always
after DOS.SYS loads.

The other chunks of glue provide externals like clear and clock
which normally come from the BIOS in ROM and TOS.IMG versions.

Once you have DOS.SYS which loads and announces itself, you debug
as follows:

While DOS.SYS is loading from floppy, hold down the left
shift key.  This causes the system to pause after the logic-hazard
announcement.  As long as you hold down the left-shift key, the
system will stay paused.  A menu appears telling what to do now:
let go of left-shift to continue, or hit right-shift to install
the debugger stub, or hit Control to install the stub AND CALL THE 
DEBUGGER RIGHT AWAY.  Ideally, you have the same DOS.SYS file
accessible on the debugger machine, so you can load symbols
from it.  To load symbols, say

	getsym dos.sys (+ `stubbp 1c)

because `stubbp gets set to the "basepage" (really just the PRG header)
for DOS.SYS, and the text base (where self.o starts) is at that
address plus $1c.

Now you have symbols, and can debug GEMDOS just like any other program.
Stop buttons come in handy here, and the file "os.db" and "doos.db" and
"doosif.db" interact (I forget just how) to let you see all OS calls,
their parameters, and what they return.  It might be slow, but it sure
is informative.
